{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg3fVbDLxhMs5raG7V5YB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sunlight looks white, but it is a mix of every visible wavelength. When it enters the atmosphere, it hits nitrogen and oxygen molecules that are much smaller than the wavelength of visible light.\n\nThese molecules scatter light through a process called Rayleigh scattering. Its strength goes up sharply as wavelength gets shorter, roughly with the inverse fourth power. So blue light is scattered several times more strongly than red and comes at us from every part of the sky.\n\nViolet is scattered even more than blue, but the sky still doesn't look violet. Sunlight contains less violet, and our eyes are less sensitive to it, so the combined light looks sky-blue. At sunrise and sunset, light passes through much more air, so most of the blue is scattered away and the remaining reds and oranges reach us."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14528,"cache_read_input_tokens":10238,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14528},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90742846-5bd2-433f-a1a7-147d685471f0","uuid":"bf753ae2-9318-4497-8a6c-5b80bfa2fd75","timestamp":"2026-10-15T07:55:55.364Z","request_id":"req_011Cg3fVawyum5tf1j61imoa"}
